  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nCyclonic low level flow will continue into this evening before weak \nmid level ridging pushes into the area overnight. With a fair amount \nof low level moisture persisting into this evening, expect areas of \nlight rain and drizzle to linger into the evening before beginning \nto taper off from west to east as we head later into the night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nMid level ridging will shift quickly off to the east on Saturday as \na weak mid level disturbance moves east across the Ohio Valley. \nDeeper moisture is lacking with this system though, so expect the \nmain result to just be some mid and high level clouds on Saturday.\n\nA strong mid level short wave will move out of the mid Mississippi \nValley on Sunday with an associated surface low lifting northeast \nfrom the mid Mississippi Valley on Sunday into the lower Great Lakes\nthrough Sunday night, with the surface low likely tracking northeast\nacross our area through Sunday evening. Good moisture advection \nahead of this will lead to widespread rain once again developing \nduring the day on Sunday and continuing into Sunday evening before \ntapering off overnight as the low lifts off to the northeast.\n\nAs of now, models are not showing much in the way of instability \nadvecting up into our area, but just based on the track of the \nsurface low, would not be surprised to see a few embedded \nthunderstorms Sunday evening, especially across our southeast. Would\nexpect the heaviest rainfall to be along and northwest of the \nsurface low track, where 1 to 1.5 inches of rain will be possible and\nas of now, it looks like this would be across northwest portions of \nour area. \n\nOn the backside of the low, developing low level CAA will lead to \ngusty northwest winds. Will nudge up from guidance a bit with wind \ngusts to around 35 mph possible Sunday evening.",
